AB  - Reactive magnesium oxide cement (RMC) is an emerging low-carbon binder with CO2-sequestration potential, but its practical use is often limited by slow hydration, which typically requires chemical hydration agents and substantial freshwater input. This study evaluates seawater as a dual-function solution: a natural, abundant hydration medium, and a practical means of eliminating freshwater use. Isothermal calorimetry and microstructural analyses show that seawater accelerates hydration relative to a 0.1 M magnesium acetate solution, leading to the formation of finer brucite crystals with higher surface area, which promotes subsequent carbonation. Under ambient-pressure accelerated carbonation (20% CO2, 80% RH, 30 degrees C), seawater-mixed mortars achieved 60% higher compressive strength (81.1 MPa) and 65% higher flexural strength (5.3 MPa) at 28 days than magnesium acetate controls. Multi-technique characterization (XRD, TGA, FT-IR, SEM-EDS, and NMR) indicates greater hydrated magnesium carbonates formation in exterior regions and, for the first time to our knowledge, anhydrous magnesite (MgCO3) formation in interior regions under ambient pressure. The presence of this most stable and densest magnesium carbonate phase contributed to matrix densification and superior late-age strength. These findings demonstrate seawater's potential as a sustainable, performanceenhancing hydration medium for RMC, providing a basis for optimizing carbonation pathways in waterstressed regions reliant on desalination and offering a mechanistic foundation for advancing RMC toward broader global applications.
