AB  - Deriving comparable estimates of oceanic phytoplankton productivity (PP) hasn't been straightforward even today, given the dissimilarities and limitations linked to every technique. We present in-situ phytoplankton productivity (PP) estimates from different domains of the Indian sector of the Southern Ocean (ISSO) with an aim to improve the sparsely addressed PP variability in this region. Comparison of PP estimates from different techniques (C-13, C-14, and fast repetition rate fluorometry, FRRf) revealed an agreement between C-13 and C-14 and a stronger correlation between C-13 and FRRf-based PP (C-13 being overestimated by a factor of 1.24) than C-14 and FRRf-based PP. The agreement between C-13-FRRf estimates indicates FRRf is a promising technique for PP studies. The study also highlights the efficacy of FRRf in knowing the phytoplankton photochemical yield and photo-physiology better at a higher spatiotemporal resolution. The observations also confirmed the moderate photosynthetic capacity of the ISSO phytoplankton, which could be due to the photo-adaptive responses to limited light in the region. Efforts in this direction would facilitate the standardization of the stable C-isotope and minimize radioactive addition in polar oceans. The active fluorometry-based technique could ease PP estimation at a more extensive scale and overcome the limitations of methods involving the labor-intensive and time-consuming incubation methods, thereby acting as a robust alternative method for obtaining PP measurements in a higher spatiotemporal resolution compared to the traditional incubation experiments in the ISSO.
