AB  - Photosynthetically driven microbially induced carbonate precipitation (MICP) has emerged as a promising pathway for metal immobilization; however, the mechanisms governing mineral formation under microalgal conditions remain poorly understood. In this study, copper immobilization was investigated using Chlorella vulgaris across a wide concentration range (0.008-50 mM Cu), combining batch experiments, adsorption controls, geochemical modeling, and FTIR characterization. Photosynthetic activity controlled pH dynamics through CO2 uptake, promoting carbonate supersaturation and mineral formation. Copper removal reached up to 90% at 0.05 mM, while abiotic precipitation dominated at both low (0.008 mM) and high concentrations (>= 1 mM), indicating a shift from biologically influenced to chemically controlled regimes. Adsorption contributed less than 10%, suggesting a secondary role primarily associated with heterogeneous nucleation. Equilibrium modeling predicted well-defined carbonate phases such as malachite, whereas FTIR analysis identified spectral features consistent with poorly crystalline, organic-associated carbonate phases and Cu-O bonding environments, supporting non-equilibrium mineralization pathways. These results demonstrate that mineral formation in photosynthetic MICP systems is governed by the coupling between microalgal activity and solution chemistry, rather than by equilibrium conditions alone. The findings highlight the importance of biological interfaces and transient physicochemical conditions in controlling mineralization pathways, with direct implications for the design and optimization of microalgae-based systems for metal removal.
