AB  - Carbon mineralization in basalt has been identified as a promising technology for quick and permanent carbon reduction. Understanding the migration and transformation of CO2 in basalt formations is crucial for evaluating the safety and efficiency of this technology. However, the spatial evolution of geochemical species and its implications for different injection strategies remain less explored. This study employed reactive transport modelling to investigate the migration of CO2 and the evolution of mineral compositions under four CO2 injection strategies: continuous gaseous CO2 (CGC), CO2-saturated water (CSW), CO2-saturated seawater (CSSW), and gaseous CO2 alternating water (GAW) in basalt formations of the Leizhou Peninsula. Results indicate that the mineralization efficiencies for CGC, CSW, CSSW, and GAW scenarios were 38.83%, 81.06%, 82.10%, and 44.22%, respectively. Lower mineralization efficiencies in the CGC and GAW scenarios were attributed to rapid CO2 migration into overlying sedimentary layers, reducing CO2 residence time in basalt. The mineralization primarily occurred at the interface between the basalt and the overlying sedimentary rock. In contrast, the CSW and CSSW injections facilitated CO2-saturated fluid migration throughout the entire basalt layer, minimizing CO2 escape and enhancing fluid-rock interaction. Among all scenarios, the CSSW achieved the highest mineralization rate and volume. Further analysis revealed that mineral dissolution predominantly involved forsterite, fayalite, magnetite, and diopside, whereas magnesite, siderite, and dolomite exhibited higher precipitation rates. These findings provide critical insights into optimizing CO2 injection strategies for efficient and long-term basaltic carbon sequestration.
