KW  - POSITIVE FEEDBACKS
KW  - CARBON STORAGE
KW  - VENICE LAGOON
KW  - BLUE CARBON
KW  - COASTAL
KW  - RECOVERY
AB  - Seagrass beds are depositional environments that support healthy estuaries and act as significant blue carbon sinks. Criteria for differentiating seagrass sedimentary units from underlying strata is lacking; however, this information is needed to accurately assess the carbon stocks of modern seagrass beds, recognize the presence of older seagrass beds that may be preserved in the stratigraphic record, and assess the resilience of seagrass habitats to burial events. This study aims to develop a seagrass depositional facies model from 24 cores, collected in a temperate, back-barrier setting. We collected four core transects along the southern part of the Outer Banks, North Carolina, U.S.A., extending perpendicularly from the back barrier shoreline of Core Banks into Core Sound across patchy and continuous seagrass meadows (Zostera marina and Halodule wrightii). Our resulting facies model demonstrates that high salinity seagrass beds consistently form a depositional sequence composed of a lower bioturbated sandy unit and an upper organic-rich muddy unit. The lower bioturbated unit and the upper organic-rich unit represent an early discontinuous seagrass bed and a subsequent well-established continuous seagrass meadow, respectively, based on the presence of these units at the surface of corresponding modern beds. Earlier seagrass units (similar to 1265-1465 CE) were buried as shifting inlets deposited flood-tidal delta sand on meadows during periods of increased storminess. We found multiple stacked seagrass depositional sequences, indicating that seagrass meadows buried by heightened storm and/or inlet activity resisted total erosion and were subsequently revegetated. Stacked seagrass sequences reflect the cyclical succession of meadow establishment, sand burial, and regrowth characteristic of the dynamic back-barrier setting. This work underscores the strong resilience of seagrass beds, and their associated carbon stocks, to disturbances such as storm burial while providing a framework for identifying carbon stocks throughout the entire seagrass depositional environment.
