AB  - Mangroves are unique intertidal ecosystems distributed along tropical and subtropical coastlines, playing a vital role in biodiversity conservation, coastal protection, and the provision of essential ecosystem services. South Asia hosts one of the world's most diverse and extensive mangrove networks, encompassing approximately 13800 km2. The region's mangroves, particularly the Sundarbans of Bangladesh and India are recognized as biodiversity hotspots, supporting more than 1500 faunas, providing storm protection, sequestering substantial amounts of carbon, and sustaining the livelihoods of over 35 million coastal inhabitants. Mangroves are facing unprecedented threats from aquaculture expansion, agricultural encroachment, deforestation, pollution, and climate change. Since 1980s, these regions has lost nearly 24% of its mangrove, primarily due to shrimp farming and land-use conversion. Bangladesh alone has lost over 1500 km2 of mangroves, while the Indus Delta of Pakistan has experienced an 86% decline, attributed to reduced freshwater inflow. Additional stressors like climate-induced sea-level rise (SLR), oil spills, salt production, heavy metal and plastic pollution further exacerbate degradation. Alongside natural regeneration is increasingly constrained by rising salinity and sediment depletion. Despite these challenges, community-based restoration initiatives in Bangladesh and Sri Lanka, along with blue carbon initiatives and coordinated regional conservation efforts offer promising pathways for recovery. This review synthesizes the current knowledge on mangrove biodiversity, ecological and economic importance, climate and pollution related threats, and ongoing conservation strategies. It highlights country-specific trends, biodiversity patterns, and policy frameworks, offering integrated management recommendations to enhance long-term resilience. Strengthening transboundary cooperation, expanding blue carbon financing, and enforcing robust environmental regulations are essential for safeguarding South Asia's mangrove heritage.
