AB  - When integrated with renewable energy systems, electrochemical carbon capture and utilization technologies enable the storage of renewable energies in carbon-containing fuels. As the pre-step of utilization, an efficient carbon capture process is extremely important for improving energy storage efficiencies. Various electrochemical carbon capture (ECC) technologies have been developed including some commonly used techniques (e.g., cationswing, redox-swing, and pH-swing method) and several newly developed methods (e.g., supercapacitor swing adsorption and charged-sorbents). This review has first summarized the timeline and principle of ECCs, and then discussed their challenges in industrial development. Particularly, we compared these ECCs with each other and discussed their advantages and disadvantages when used in various scenarios, including flue gas capture, direct air capture, and direct ocean capture. Finally, we give a perspective for the possible future developing direction of ECCs.
