TI  - Superstructure-based marine waste-to-fuel production with air-pollutant utilization: Techno-economic and life-cycle assessment across countries
T2  - CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA  - English
KW  - Marine waste
KW  - Waste-to-fuel
KW  - Air pollutant capture
KW  - Techno-economic analysis
KW  - Life-cycle assessment
KW  - CO2 CAPTURE
AB  - Large-scale marine waste discharge creates severe ecological and economic burdens. Marine wastes - hydrocarbon-rich waste plastics, alkaline waste seashells, and desalination wastewater - can be converted into fuels and chemicals while simultaneously reducing and utilizing air pollutants. This study developed a marine waste-to-fuel framework based on combinations of the three waste streams and air-pollutant capture/use routes. Techno-economic analysis and life-cycle assessment across five countries show that, excluding Spain, pathway deployment can cut costs by 20%-60% relative to current disposal practices and reduce net carbon emissions by up to 30%. Additionally, recovering carbonates and sulfates from air pollutants mitigates mineral depletion impacts. Viability varies by country, driven by waste discharge rates, fuel and hydrogen market prices, and energy costs. Sensitivity analyses identify electricity price as the dominant economic lever and show that carbon pricing can shift the preferred pathway, underscoring the need for flexible strategies aligned with evolving policy and market conditions.
