AB  - The development of terrestrial vegetation and its impact on silicate weathering is hypothesized to have driven multiple cooling events throughout the Paleozoic. One such event is the 8-10 degrees C cooling observed from the late Silurian to early Devonian (c. 420-385 Ma). Chemical weathering of Ca- and Mg-bearing silicate minerals and the subsequent trapping of carbon in marine carbonates are a sink for atmospheric CO2 on multimillion-year time scales. The strontium isotopic ratio (Sr-87/Sr-86) of seawater acts as a geochemical proxy for this process. In this study, we present new Silurian to Devonian Sr-87/Sr-86 data paired with oxygen isotopes (delta O-18; a proxy for palaeotemperature and ice volume) from conodont apatite. The two datasets show an inflection in the late Silurian to early Devonian, suggesting a connection between enhanced basaltic weathering (leading to lower Sr-87/Sr-86) and cooling (increasing delta O-18). The spread of terrestrial plants probably drove a significant portion of the enhanced basaltic weathering, which would have driven cooling, in addition to coeval tectonic changes in the Pridoli Epoch (422.7-419.0 Ma) and Lochkovian Age that further enhanced weathering and drawdown of atmospheric CO2 (419.0-412.4 Ma).
