AB  - Electrochemical hydrogen looping (EHL) was previously shown as a promising approach for direct ocean capture, combining pH-swing CO2 release with theoretical hydrogen self-sufficiency. In this work, a systematic reactor engineering optimization of an EHL flow cell is presented with the objective of minimizing electrochemical energy consumption (EEC) while maintaining high capture efficiency. Key reactor parameters including membrane support structures, interelectrode distance, membrane thickness and configuration, and anodic substrate material were independently evaluated under controlled conditions using synthetic seawater. Mechanical stabilization of the membranes, by means of a middle compartment support structure, was found to be essential for stable operation, while reducing interelectrode distance and optimizing membrane thickness significantly lowered the cell voltage without compromising capture efficiency. Further performance gains were achieved by eliminating the anodic membrane and replacing the carbon-based anode substrate with a corrosion-resistant Ptcoated titanium felt. Collectively, these optimizations reduced the EEC from 205.0 kJ mol(-1) CO2 to 117.3 kJ mol(-1) CO2 at 20 mA cm(-2), while preserving capture efficiencies above 75%. These results demonstrate that targeted reactor-level design improvements can substantially enhance the energy efficiency, durability, and scalability of EHL-based direct ocean capture systems to go toward industrial feasibility.
