AB  - Delta C-14(atm) has been estimated as 420 +/- 80 parts per thousand (IntCal09) during the Last Glacial Maximum (LGM) compared to preindustrial times (0 parts per thousand), but mechanisms explaining this difference are not yet resolved. Delta C-14(atm) is a function of both cosmogenic production in the high atmosphere and of carbon cycling and partitioning in the Earth system. Be-10-based reconstructions show a contribution of the cosmogenic production term of only 200 +/- 200 parts per thousand in the LGM. The remaining 220 parts per thousand have thus to be explained by changes in the carbon cycle. Recently, Bouttes et al. (2010, 2011) proposed to explain most of the difference in pCO(2atm) and delta C-13 between glacial and interglacial times as a result of brine-induced ocean stratification in the Southern Ocean. This mechanism involves the formation of very saline water masses that contribute to high carbon storage in the deep ocean. During glacial times, the sinking of brines is enhanced and more carbon is stored in the deep ocean, lowering pCO(2atm). Moreover, the sinking of brines induces increased stratification in the Southern Ocean, which keeps the deep ocean well isolated from the surface. Such an isolated ocean reservoir would be characterized by a low Delta C-14 signature. Evidence of such C-14-depleted deep waters during the LGM has recently been found in the Southern Ocean (Skinner et al. 2010). The degassing of this carbon with low Delta C-14 would then reduce Delta C-14(atm) throughout the deglaciation. We have further developed the CLIMBER-2 model to include a cosmogenic production of C-14 as well as an interactive atmospheric C-14 reservoir. We investigate the role of both the sinking of brine and cosmogenic production, alongside iron fertilization mechanisms, to explain changes in Delta C-14(atm) during the last deglaciation. In our simulations, not only is the sinking of brine mechanism consistent with past Delta C-14 data, but it also explains most of the differences in pCO(2atm) and Delta C-14(atm) between the LGM and preindustrial times. Finally, this study represents the first time to our knowledge that a model experiment explains glacial-interglacial differences in pCO(2atm), delta C-13, and Delta C-14 together with a coherent LGM climate.
