AB  - Selective recovery of rare earth elements (REEs) from complex aqueous streams is important for circular resource use, yet remains difficult in multi-cation systems where ion removal is often governed by non-specific electrical double-layer adsorption. In this study, a polymer-functionalized MnO2@graphene oxide (PMG) composite was used as a flow-electrode material in a flow-electrode capacitive deionization (FCDI) system to improve REE selectivity under acidic multi-ion matrices and acid-adjusted brine reference conditions. MnO2 provided redoxactive intercalation sites, while the polymer introduced coordination-capable functional groups and improved acid stability. GO served mainly as a conductive scaffold that improved particle dispersion and interfacial accessibility. Across synthetic acid mine drainage (AMD), seawater brine, and permanent magnet leachate, PMG exhibited superior performance compared to activated carbon (AC), achieving alpha(REE/Fe3+) = 24.9, alpha(REE/ Ca2+) = 22.2, and alpha(REE/Co2+) = 60.4, with REE removal efficiencies of 81-91%. PMG reduced the recovery-normalized specific energy consumption by 83-91% relative to AC, depending on the feed matrix. Despite its low surface area (12.23 m2/g), PMG showed higher selectivity than AC, suggesting that the separation behavior was not governed primarily by surface-area-driven ion storage. XPS analysis showed Mn valence redistribution and changes in nitrogen bonding states after operation, consistent with a redox-coordination-mediated uptake pathway. The polymer coating suppressed Mn2+ dissolution by 93.5% at pH 3.5 and maintained stable performance over 50 cycles. Coupling the desorption stream with hydroxide precipitation enabled 82.5% recovery of REEs at 87.3% purity. These results support the feasibility of selective and energy-efficient electrochemical REE recovery from complex aqueous matrices.
